WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:12.269
Writer/Director/Producer Joe Badon: When working
with 13 days and like $8,000, you are talking

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:19.550
about just a few hundred dollars a day that
you have for everything you’re doing.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:30.480
And so you don’t have time to do pick-ups,
you don’t have time to set up a shot once

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.480
you get there.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:38.120
You need to make sure you know exactly what
you’re going to be doing that day.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.760
So when I go to finishing the script…I’m
an illustrator.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.500
That’s what I did for a long, comic books
and children’s books and I did storyboards

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:55.329
for movies and I saw the importance of getting
the storyboards down so that when you get

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:59.420
to the set, you’re not wasting time.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.460
I storyboarded the entire script from the
very first shot to the very last shot.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.080
And I knew it was going to be important.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.440
But I didn’t realize how important it was
until once we got there.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.460
So my first AD Aria Mickenberg was able to
open up the book, look at the shot we needed

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.890
and say “We need this shot” and we go
“Okay, let’s set up the shot.”

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:26.470
Dan Waghorne our DP, sets the shot up, sets
the lights up, shoots it, we do a red circle

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:27.470
around that…

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:28.740
“What’s the next shot?”

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:34.380
Right here, right in front of us because we
have the storyboard illustrated.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:44.509
And that was far and above a huge, important
piece of the puzzle to get the movie done

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:52.060
in time, on budget and finished without having
to go back and go “Oh…we totally forgot

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.220
this shot!”

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.180
Now we have to find the location again and
find the actors again and set up this time.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.159
We didn’t have to do that because we looked
at the the storyboards and we had finished

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:04.159
it all.

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:06.360
Editor, Joseph Estrade: I didn’t expect
to walk into the first meeting of the film

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.070
and immediately know what the film was going
to look like in the end (which was very important).

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.700
And now that I am editing the film, I don’t
really know what I would have done without

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:21.500
it because every time I go into a scene, the
first thing I do is look at all the storyboards

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.010
to get a feeling for what Joe wants out of
the scene.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.760
And knowing that, having that backbone, that
structure, allows me to kind of branch off

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.449
of it and to build off of it and to be more
creative because I can allocate more of my

00:02:33.449 --> 00:02:39.519
mind and my time to the little details as
opposed to the backbone structure.

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:41.990
Cinematographer, Daniel Waghorne:&nbsp; I think
the storyboards are one of the most important

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.380
parts of making sure this film got made on
time.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:52.040
I mean we shot 90 pages in 13 days, which
was kind of insane and I just remember the

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:58.079
first time that we were presented with storyboards,
I looked at this and said “This is going

00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:00.579
to make my job a million times easier.”

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:07.220
The movie was basically already made and all
we had to do was just get in there and actually

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.500
shoot it and even though we did change up
a few things here or there, at least we still

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.290
had the foundation of everything already built.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:20.799
So I was able to see exactly what Joe wanted
to make and that made everybody’s life a

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:21.589
hundred times easier.

